WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:07.220
For Alan, hanging up the Chinese character
for double happiness is an ironic gesture.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.710
It’s usually reserved for newly weds, but
Alan is single -- and she doesn’t want to

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.710
be a wife.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:20.210
What she does want is to be a mother.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:30.070
"I've wanted to have a baby my whole adult
life.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:35.330
I want to be a mother and as a woman I want
to experience getting pregnant and giving

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.590
birth."

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:46.450
Alan has petitioned delegates of the National
People's Congress, China's Parliament, asking

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:52.140
that single women be given access to sperm
banks -- a taboo in China, where family planning

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.790
authorities can still fine unmarried mothers
for unsanctioned babies.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:02.160
She’s realistic about her chances of immediate
success.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.850
"Getting access to artificial reproduction
technology as an unmarried woman is certainly

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.999
going to very difficult.

00:01:10.999 --> 00:01:14.869
But I think some time in the very, very distant
future, it will be possible."

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:20.450
Alan, not her real name, has talked about
her yearning for motherhood on social media.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.420
Her posts have sparked a lively debate in
a society where traditional family values

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.420
still rule.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.310
In a park in Beijing, parents gather on Sundays
to lay out their children’s resumes for

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.290
prospective partners.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.640
An unmarried child can stigmatize the whole
family.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.159
Women in particular are under huge pressure
to marry by their late twenties.

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:46.939
"Marriage in China is an arrangement between
families.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:50.700
Everything that happens in that marriage becomes
the business of the couple’s parents and

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.380
extended family.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.454
But I want a relationship where two people
decide for themselves whether they want to

00:01:55.454 --> 00:02:01.759
be together, how to live and whether they
want children."

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:05.579
Alan known those aspirations won’t make
it easy to find a partner.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:08.250
So she's now looking for a sperm donor online.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.250
Currently, she holds down three different
jobs in the creative industry.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:18.020
She belongs to China's "me' generation" -- those
born after 1990 and often considered more

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.409
individualistic and demanding.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:20.409
"Our generation's ideas about marriage and
childbirth are very far away from the previous

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:21.409
generations.

00:02:21.409 --> 00:02:22.409
Most people don’t discuss these issues.

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:23.409
But if you look at their behavior, there is
a clear message: And that message is that

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.409
our generation is less ready to compromise
and wants to have more personal choices."

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:25.750
Alan says she would love to find a father
who would take care of the child, without

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.349
the expectations of a traditional family.

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:32.200
But if that doesn’t work out, she is willing
to go it all alone.

